    "result_text": "The full test suite is green: **Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0**.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe requirement: all rejection responses (400, 409, 429) must return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`.\n\n**What I found:** OfficeFloor writes responses by delegating to Spring MVC's return-value handlers (`SpringServerHttpConnection` \u2192 `RequestMappingHandlerAdapter.getReturnValueHandlers()`), whose `HttpEntityMethodProcessor.writeWithMessageConverters` automatically sets `Content-Type: application/problem+json` when the body is a Spring `ProblemDetail`. The 500/404/403 and the `MethodArgumentNotValid` (400) handlers already used this pattern via the existing `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper. But the nine business-rule rejection handlers were still emitting plain `Map.of(\"errors\", ...)` JSON.\n\n**What I changed** \u2014 converted these handlers to return `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>` built with the existing `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper (which sets `type` = `about:blank`, `title` = exception simple name, `status`, and `detail`), keeping their HTTP status codes and preserving the field info as an `errors` extension member:\n\n- **400:** `MissingOwnerFields`, `DisposableEmail`, `FutureRegistrationDate`, `InvalidEmail`, `InvalidPostcode`, `InvalidTelephone`\n- **409:** `CityAtCapacity`, `DuplicateIdentity`\n- **429:** `DailyOwnerLimitExceeded`\n\nThe new acceptance test (`coreRejectionIsProblemJson`, which drops `city` \u2192 400) now passes, asserting `application/problem+json` content type and `$.status == 400`. I also confirmed no other code paths outside the escalation handlers emit these statuses.",
